::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of f(x) = 4x<sup>2</sup> - 7x + 1? {
~4 #This is the leading coefficient, not the degree.
=2 #Correct. The highest exponent is 2, so this quadratic has degree 2 rather than using a coefficient or constant.
~7 #This is part of the linear coefficient.
~1 #This is the constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic has vertex (3, -6) and opens upward. What is its minimum value? {
~3 #This is the x-coordinate where the minimum occurs.
~-3 #This changes the sign of the x-coordinate.
=-6 #Correct. The y-coordinate of the vertex is the minimum value.
~6 #This changes the sign of the minimum value.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement describes the end behavior of f(x) = -2x<sup>5</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> - 1? {
~Both ends rise. #That would fit an even degree with positive leading coefficient.
~Both ends fall. #That would fit an even degree with negative leading coefficient.
~Falls left and rises right. #That would fit an odd degree with positive leading coefficient.
=Rises left and falls right. #Correct. Odd degree with negative leading coefficient rises left and falls right.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x - 4)<sup>2</sup>(x + 1), which zero has even multiplicity? {
=x = 4 #Correct. The repeated factor (x - 4)<sup>2</sup> gives even multiplicity.
~x = -1 #This zero comes from a factor with exponent 1.
~x = -4 #The sign is reversed for the factor x - 4.
~x = 1 #The sign is reversed for the factor x + 1.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature is most useful for sketching f(x) = (x + 2)(x - 5)? {
~The y-intercept is 5. #The y-intercept is f(0) = -10, not 5.
=Zeros at x = -2 and x = 5 #Correct. Setting x + 2 = 0 and x - 5 = 0 gives the two x-intercepts for the sketch.
~Vertex at (-2, 5) #The factors do not directly give this vertex.
~No x-intercepts #The factored form shows two x-intercepts.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial model H(t) has H(0) = 12. What does 12 represent? {
~A zero of the model #A zero happens when the output is 0.
~The degree of the polynomial #The value H(0) does not identify degree.
=The initial output value #Correct. H(0) is the y-intercept or starting output.
~The right-end behavior #End behavior describes what happens as t becomes very large.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which interval solves x<sup>2</sup> - 16 <= 0? {
~-4 < x < 4 #This excludes the boundary values, but the inequality includes equality.
~x <= -4 or x >= 4 #That is where the graph is on or above the x-axis.
~-16 <= x <= 16 #These values come from the constant, not the zeros.
=-4 <= x <= 4 #Correct. The graph is on or below the x-axis between and including the zeros.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.5<br><br>Question:<br>For y &gt; x<sup>2</sup> - 3, how should the boundary be drawn? {
~Solid, because the graph is a parabola #Boundary style depends on equality, not graph type.
=Dashed, because the inequality is strict #Correct. The symbol &gt; does not include equality.
~Solid, because the inequality is strict #Strict inequalities use dashed boundaries.
~No boundary is needed #A two-variable inequality needs a boundary graph.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y <= -x<sup>2</sup> + 4, where is the solution region? {
~Above the parabola #Above would match y greater than the expression.
~Only at the vertex #The solution region includes many points, not only the vertex.
=Below the parabola, including the boundary #Correct. The symbol <= means y-values less than or equal to the boundary.
~Outside the x-intercepts only #That describes a one-variable interval, not the full region.
}

::ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_Pretest_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 2 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial graph touches and turns at x = -3, crosses at x = 2, and falls to the right. Which statement is most reasonable? {
~x = -3 has odd multiplicity and x = 2 has even multiplicity. #Touching suggests even multiplicity; crossing suggests odd multiplicity.
~The leading coefficient must be positive. #Falling to the right suggests a negative leading coefficient for odd degree behavior.
~There are no real zeros. #The graph has real zeros at -3 and 2.
=x = -3 likely has even multiplicity, and x = 2 likely has odd multiplicity. #Correct. Touching indicates even multiplicity and crossing indicates odd multiplicity.
}

